from this enginner critisizm This build is much better than your first screenshots. The ground now spans the full width, the per-era HUDs differ, each era has its own landmarks, the hint text is gone, and there’s a story layer. It’s no longer an obvious “shapes plus filters” scene. It still has a handful of AI and procedural tells. I only saw the first 300 frames of each stage with a fixed seed, so I can’t judge pacing, bosses, audio or game feel.
Biggest tells (all stages)

The dirt is a rigid tiled grid.
In 8-bit, 16-bit, 32-bit and 2.5D, the pebbles sit at identical spacing, alternating rows, and the green ticks repeat about every 100px. It reads as a pattern stamp, not hand-drawn earth.
Fix: make 3–4 ground tile variants with different stones, roots and cracks, and place them randomly. Use dark roots and darker-green moss under the grass lip. Add a bigger irregular feature (a buried bone, a root, a fossil) every few screens.
The scenery is copy-pasted.
The 16-bit pines are the same shape in rows. The 32-bit lamp posts are evenly spaced. The enemy blob and the two pickups are the same in every era.
Fix: vary scale, flip and hue per instance. Redraw the enemy and pickup for each era’s visual language. Also check that the fixed platform position I saw in every shot comes from the fixed seed and not from the layout generator.
Mixed pixel densities.
In 32-bit the clouds are chunky 8px blocks, while windows and signs are 1–2px.
In 2.5D and 3D a low-resolution pixel backdrop sits behind smooth 3D models.
Fix: one logical resolution per era, with whole-number scaling. In the 3D era, render the backdrop at the same resolution as the models, or pixelate everything consistently.
Generic palette and stock shapes.
The 8-bit stage is saturated stock blue, lavender mountains and lime grass. The 8-bit stage also uses far more colours than the NES-style limit its label promises, so 8-bit and 16-bit feel the same apart from detail.
The 8-bit bricks are red and orange like Mario’s bricks, so they feel derivative.
Fix: cap the palette for 8-bit (4 colours per sprite). Hue-shift shadows to blue or purple and highlights to yellow. Give the platform a material that fits each memory: washing-line board, crate, camp planks, bridge girder.
Platforms don’t connect to the world.
They float with no support. In 4-shade the board merges with the shirt behind it. In 32-bit the blue slab looks like a hole in the viaduct. The 2.5D grey box has stretched oval “lens” highlights that look like a placeholder texture.
Fix: give platforms a different value and outline from the background, plus a drop shadow. Redo the 2.5D box texture with proper bevels.
The world and the characters use different rules.
In 3D the world has black toon outlines, but Gran and Sam are unoutlined, textured billboards. The sun is a blurry pixel blob behind crisp models.
Fix: one outline rule for everything, or none.

Per-screen notes
Title screen

The tagline and the “Press any button” line sit on top of the dotted scenery and are hard to read. Dim, pulsing text on busy dots is the worst case.
The controls lines are tiny, and the orange and blue text is low contrast.
The “GameJam 2026 . theme: Time” footer reads as a dev placeholder.
Fix: put a solid panel or darkened band behind the text, keep the scenery away from the centre, and drop the footer.

Story cards

The polaroid is rotated by a fixed angle, which breaks pixel art with jagged, shimmering edges. Every card uses the same tilt and the same tape.
Fix: snap the rotation to 0 or draw a pre-rotated sprite, and vary the tilt per card.
The caption repeats the title (“Sam’s first day at school” twice).
Fix: use a different caption, for example a hand-written note or a date stamp.
Apostrophes render as an acute accent (Sam´s, Alzheimer´s), and the opening quote mark has no matching closing mark.
Fix: a font glyph or mapping bug.
The text column starts at a different x on each card (about 167, 219 and 148px), and the present-day card has a huge empty lower half.
Fix: use one grid for all cards.
The text is cut mid-word in some shots (“called h”, “right here at”). This is probably the typewriter effect caught mid-reveal, so check that it finishes and can be skipped.
Dim timeline labels and “FURTHER BACK” are low contrast on the near-black.

Stage 1 (1-bit)

The dotted outline scenery is at the same brightness as the characters, so there’s no depth, and it looks like a broken-line artefact. The bottom 20% is empty black.
Fix: draw solid white silhouettes with dither only on large areas.

Stage 2 (4-shade)

The clothesline idea is strong. The shirts are larger than the characters.
The dark tree crown is random checker noise, and the clouds are near-identical stamps.
Fix: hand-draw the tree crown with clustered shapes, and cut the noise.

Stage 3 (8-bit)

Stray dark pixels sit along the mountain ridges.
The school sign is cropped to “SC” at the edge, and a cloud is cut off under the HUD.

Stage 4 (16-bit)

There’s a visible dithered seam running across the whole screen at one height, and vertical jagged streaks in the mountains.
The pines are cloned.
The HUD boxes cover the tree canopy, so the best prop is cut off at the top.
Pillarbox black bars appear here but not in 8-bit.

Stage 5 (32-bit)

It’s the strongest stage. The viaduct and train are good.
The shop signs (“ERY”, “FE”) are cropped or covered by the characters.
Mixed pixel sizes are most obvious here, as noted above.

Stage 6 (2.5D)

The bottom third and the mid-field are flat, featureless green, so the screen looks unfinished.
Pixel backdrop and smooth ground clash.
The mountain silhouette shows through the dark HUD panels.

Stage 7 (3D)

Gran and Sam overlap almost exactly in my capture. That might just be where they stood at frame 300, but check it.
The bottom third is empty green, a lamp post pokes through the platform, and a stray white dash sits on the roof.

General gripes

Characters are small, about 7% of screen height. Gran and Sam blend into busy backgrounds, especially in 4-shade and 16-bit. Add a 1px outline and make them about 1.5× bigger.
HUD inconsistency. The HUD moves between top and bottom, and the era name and score are dim grey-blue on dark. The HUD takes about 12% of the screen in the top three eras, and it covers the scenery.
Font legibility. The squared “N” reads like “H” (GRAN, NOW), and the “Y” and “4” are ambiguous. Use a more legible pixel font for small text, and keep the quirky one for titles.
Pillarboxing and borders vary by era (thin borders in some, big bars in others). It reads as a bug, not a deliberate era change.
Dead space in the lower third of 1-bit, 2.5D and 3D. Fill it with foreground props, grass, water or fog.

Most of the engineer’s list is fixed:

Ground and platforms: the ground has varied tiles with roots, moss and buried keepsakes. Each memory has its own platform: books, a camp log, a girder, a hay bale.
Characters: they’re 1.5x bigger, and the enemies are redrawn per stage.
Text: small text uses a new readable font, and the HUD is at the top in every stage.
Title and story cards: the title text sits on a solid panel and the footer is gone. The story cards are fixed.
2.5D and 3D: the empty lower third is filled.

